<p>The conflict involving Iran has sharpened fears of a prolonged global energy shock. International Energy Agency Executive Director Fatih Birol warned that the fallout would not fade quickly even if the fighting stopped, calling it a &quot;major, major threat&quot; to the global economy. Another immediate concern is the Gulf&#x27;s concentrated energy system. Gulf states depend on tightly clustered electricity, oil-production, and export infrastructure, leaving pipelines, terminals, and power assets exposed if the war widens. Analysts are also watching ripple effects in natural gas and transport, with higher fuel costs likely to reshape markets beyond crude alone.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/gwct3mIL-uoWYWBmBoTTQDRaCUHd-qxdcgygRSm3q-7qQsnfPy4poqXP_nCTmZkfF-XpVoTClzyZlCcKs_JfqqWYwwy969zt-iwqUJ57VmrS095HiwftJufVJKsxDf5RXD41ZMzrL1_zk5LoIqnAOPT-onK7fpY9oDt_km6dPhhW-6r3AVrLlviOFOezfHLIdETkzcCfHC5AXX04QVqeeYqU8KPbcUEjKUuCsuCDoNxm2IVhYJmH1GsHJMYmijyLvdI2xwfq7vahGjuPoMPg8Fsy8xiajwgXcPEJfV0qOfqT-U3lRNfo9FA-qCFDZQlcmY--BM8WBd9GhUpiYMGXXNxvVETbdAX8hNOhCgSs0GBTdvhYIuqaPMCIiMhvuUeDt-CvcLMZ5cky9bUKWlc97MriMS8qalnTqc6xMScWLMG7s98Ewrwb71pzKhhJyxIhZPGl96D1MNTcT9iQB__lFU339w' alt='Image from reporting on the risks facing Gulf infrastructure during the Iran crisis.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Oil-price shock: Wood Mackenzie cited an &quot;eye-watering&quot; 50 per cent surge in global oil prices so far this month and argued that sustained fuel-cost pain could make EVs more attractive.</li><li>Hormuz scenario: The same analysis said a closure of the Strait of Hormuz could be a &quot;game-changer&quot; for EV adoption by driving conventional fuel costs sharply higher.</li><li>Gas-market squeeze: Forecasts cited by one source say many countries could reach a liquefied natural gas supply &quot;cliff edge&quot; if the conflict keeps tightening fuel flows.</li><li>Interdependence risk: In the Gulf, electricity networks and oil systems are closely linked, so damage to one set of assets could quickly cascade into the other.</li></ul><blockquote>Major, major threat - Fatih Birol</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>International Energy Agency: Fatih Birol warned that the energy fallout would not ease quickly even if the war ended, underscoring concern about lasting damage to global supply and prices. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/23/world/middleeast/energy-crisis-iea-warning.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>The Globe and Mail analysis said Gulf Arab states&#x27; reliance on electricity and oil infrastructure leaves the region especially exposed if those assets are hit during the conflict. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-why-the-stakes-are-high-for-gulf-infrastructure-in-the-iran-crisis/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Wood Mackenzie: David Brown, director of energy transition research, said a closure of the Strait of Hormuz could become a turning point for EV adoption by making oil much more expensive. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/china-economy/article/3347583/why-iran-war-could-be-game-changer-evs-and-chinas-car-industry'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/23/world/middleeast/energy-crisis-iea-warning.html'>Energy Crisis Will Not Be Resolved Quickly if War Ends, I.E.A. Chief Warns</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-why-the-stakes-are-high-for-gulf-infrastructure-in-the-iran-crisis/'>Why the stakes are high for Gulf infrastructure in the Iran crisis</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/03/24/nggw-m24.html'>Impact of Iran war on global economy intensifies daily</a> - wsws.org</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/economy/china-economy/article/3347583/why-iran-war-could-be-game-changer-evs-and-chinas-car-industry'>Why the Iran war could be a ‘game-changer’ for EVs – and China’s car industry</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='htt